    lookupname = malloc(bytes_written + 1);
    ...
    u_strToUTF8(lookupname, bytes_written, NULL, utf16_dst, utf16_dst_length, &status);
    ...
    lookupname[bytes_written] = 0;
} else {
    if (!(lookupname = strdup(lookupname)))
        goto cleanup;
}
```

Hmm, this looks safe. The `u_strToUTF8` writes at most `sizeof(lookupname_buf)` bytes to `lookupname_buf[128]`. If `bytes_written >= 128`, it mallocs and redoes. 

Wait, but there's a subtle issue. Let me reconsider. `u_strToUTF8(lookupname, sizeof(lookupname_buf), &bytes_written, ...)`. If the output needs exactly 128 bytes, `u_strToUTF8` writes 128 bytes and sets `U_STRING_NOT_TERMINATED_WARNING` (warning, not error). `bytes_written = 128`. `!U_SUCCESS`? Warning → true. `bytes_written >= 128` → true. malloc 129, redo with capacity 128. Writes 128 bytes, sets warning. `lookupname[128] = 0`. Fine.

